some gamers aren't happy about how DLC  has become synonymous with expansion  packs now technically DLC can include  expansion packs but in common usage  that's a pretty crucial difference  between the two  DLC places the emphasis on smaller  individual extra pieces of contents  generally consisting of new gear a few  new quests or missions and cosmetic  stuff  comparatively there's quite a bit more  expected out of expansion packs which  traditionally use the framework of the  base game to pursue new gameplay or  narrative ideas my first experience with  expansion packs was with Heroes of Might  &amp; Magic and the original Sims game where  developers would periodically release  meaty expansions onto store shelves  these expansions were huge with dozens  of hours of content which frequently  included totally different ways to play  the game like new factions playable  races and storylines for RPGs or a heap  of new campaign scenarios for strategy  games this hasn't all been left in the  past though Blizzard are still famously  rolling out major expansion packs for  Diablo Starcraft and World of Warcraft  which have been pretty successful at  drawing players back into these series  Starcraft 2 is just about to get another  one for example the game's first major  expansion heart of the swarm landed two  years ago and its second expansion  legacy of the void is currently in beta  testing mode these sorts of expansions  generally cost quite a few of your  dollars but for a game you're already  into they can be an excellent way to put  a whole new face on a title or franchise  without sacrificing the framework you  liked to begin with that said expansions  are certainly less prevalent than they  used to be and tends to exclusively crop  up to the more PC friendly to play games  like Skyrim civilization and XCOM DLC on  the other hand runs a broad gamut from  minor cosmetic additions like character  skins and extra weapons all the way up  to excellent  stories like The Last of Us is left  behind and Bioshock twos Minerva's Den  DLC tends to be pick and choose extra  content where you grab whatever specific  content appeals to you where expansion  packs will usually run you about 20 or  30 bucks for a huge chunk of content  some gamers get frustrated with DLC that  provides less content for a similar  price if you're a person who spends  money chances are you want to get good  value out of whatever you purchase  that's not always the case  with DLC recently unhappy feelings were  brought about when Evolved launched with  over a hundred and thirty bucks worth of  day one weapon skins and when Tiger  Woods PGA Tour 13 revealed that the  majority of its courses were hidden find  a paywall way back in the early days of  deals see the Elder Scrolls oblivion  offered a set of purely cosmetic horse  armor for two dollars fifty a move which  made a lot of people angry and which  spiraled into becoming the go-to name  for outrageous DLC offerings but there's  the vice-president Pete Hines later said  they learned a bunch of lessons from  that poor move saying it's not about the  amount of money it's about are you  giving them really good value for what  you're making them pay for we have to  make sure we're providing enough quality  for what you're paying for whatever  you're paying for so that the customer  feels satisfied that I got good value  for my money what all these examples and  many more have proved is that there are  good and bad ways to ask your fans to  spend more money on your game pay2win  strategies that instantly unlock things  a gamer would otherwise be working  toward a pretty unpopular expensive  cosmetics are also pretty unpopular  recently The Witcher 3 developers laid  out the thinking behind their plans for  DLC and expansions after those previous  examples it seems they could be on the  right track set a Projekt RED CEO masa  Nowitzki said quote first and foremost I  think the word DLC has become extremely  devalued any additional content is  called DLC whether it's one sword  awesome question  options or a full expansion pack I  really look at it differently the mini  DLC is the smaller bits and pieces and  we will never charge for those things  however if we do a big adventure say 15  or 20 hours long a very high production  value story extension to the game then  we will probably charge for that and set  a project are doing just that they're  providing 16 pieces of smaller DLC for  free which include things like extra  side missions costume changes and yes  even horse armor then they're charging  25 bucks for their expansion pass which  grants access to The Witcher threes two  expansion packs which they've said will  add up to about 30 hours of content  hopefully their strategy ends up being  as reasonable as it sounds game  publishers will always vary with how  much value for money they think they're  offering up with their DLC and expansion  packs so it may not always seem  reasonable that said it's been up to you  to decide what's worth your while as  crazy as it may sound some people are  totally up for paying 15 bucks to unlock  all the chests in Assassin's Creed unity  all the bonuses in Mortal Kombat X  otherwise those options wouldn't keep  existing other people don't care about  extra guns costumes or side quests if  they don't significantly affect the base  game and as good as Mass Effect 3 DLC  campaign missions work some people like  me decided that their time with Shepherd  was over as soon as they hit that blue  green or red light riddles end sequence  what kind of DLC and expansion packs do  you think are worth paying for  what's off-limits and what's been your  favorite let me know down in the  comments and I'll catch you again next  week
 

